    {
      "category": "curriculum",
      "field": "description",
      "value": "Welfare economics uses microeconomics techniques to evaluate well-being from allocation of productive factors as to desirability and economic efficiency within an economy, often relative to competitive general equilibrium.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": null,
      "metadata": {
        "chunk_id": "page_0023",
        "chunk_title": "Welfare",
        "chunk_order": 22,
        "source_type": "page"
      }
    },
    {
      "category": "curriculum",
      "field": "analysis_focus",
      "value": "It analyses social welfare, however measured, in terms of economic activities of the individuals that compose the theoretical society considered.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": null,
      "metadata": {
        "chunk_id": "page_0023",
        "chunk_title": "Welfare",
        "chunk_order": 22,
        "source_type": "page"
      }
    },
    {
      "category": "curriculum",
      "field": "concept",
      "value": "Accordingly, individuals, with associated economic activities, are the basic units for aggregating to social welfare, whether of a group, a community, or a society.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": null,
      "metadata": {
        "chunk_id": "page_0023",
        "chunk_title": "Welfare",
        "chunk_order": 22,
        "source_type": "page"
      }
    },
    {
      "category": "curriculum",
      "field": "social_welfare_concept",
      "value": "There is no 'social welfare' apart from the 'welfare' associated with its individual units.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": null,
      "metadata": {
        "chunk_id": "page_0023",
        "chunk_title": "Welfare",
        "chunk_order": 22,
        "source_type": "page"
      }
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "field": "definition",
      "value": "Growth economics studies factors that explain economic growth – the increase in output per capita of a country over a long period of time.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": null,
      "metadata": {
        "chunk_id": "page_0025",
        "chunk_title": "Growth",
        "chunk_order": 24,
        "source_type": "page"
      }
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "field": "focus",
      "value": "The same factors are used to explain differences in the level of output per capita between countries, in particular why some countries grow faster than others, and whether countries converge at the same rates of growth.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": null,
      "metadata": {
        "chunk_id": "page_0025",
        "chunk_title": "Growth",
        "chunk_order": 24,
        "source_type": "page"
      }
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "field": "factors_studied",
      "value": "Much-studied factors include the rate of investment, population growth, and technological change.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": null,
      "metadata": {
        "chunk_id": "page_0025",
        "chunk_title": "Growth",
        "chunk_order": 24,
        "source_type": "page"
      }
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "field": "theoretical_forms",
      "value": "These are represented in theoretical forms as in the neoclassical and endogenous growth models.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": null,
      "metadata": {
        "chunk_id": "page_0025",
        "chunk_title": "Growth",
        "chunk_order": 24,
        "source_type": "page"
      }
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "field": "empirical_forms",
      "value": "These are represented in empirical forms in growth accounting.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": null,
      "metadata": {
        "chunk_id": "page_0025",
        "chunk_title": "Growth",
        "chunk_order": 24,
        "source_type": "page"
      }
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "field": "definition",
      "value": "Governments implement fiscal policy to influence macroeconomic conditions by adjusting spending and taxation policies to alter aggregate demand.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": null,
      "metadata": {
        "chunk_id": "page_0029",
        "chunk_title": "Fiscal policy",
        "chunk_order": 28,
        "source_type": "page"
      }
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "field": "output_gap",
      "value": "When aggregate demand falls below the economy's potential output, there is an output gap in which some productive capacity remains idle.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": null,
      "metadata": {
        "chunk_id": "page_0029",
        "chunk_title": "Fiscal policy",
        "chunk_order": 28,
        "source_type": "page"
      }
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "field": "government_action",
      "value": "Governments increase spending and cut taxes to boost aggregate demand.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": null,
      "metadata": {
        "chunk_id": "page_0029",
        "chunk_title": "Fiscal policy",
        "chunk_order": 28,
        "source_type": "page"
      }
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "field": "example",
      "value": "For example, unemployed home builders can be hired to expand highways.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": null,
      "metadata": {
        "chunk_id": "page_0029",
        "chunk_title": "Fiscal policy",
        "chunk_order": 28,
        "source_type": "page"
      }
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "field": "tax_cuts_effect",
      "value": "Tax cuts allow consumers to increase their spending, which boosts aggregate demand.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": null,
      "metadata": {
        "chunk_id": "page_0029",
        "chunk_title": "Fiscal policy",
        "chunk_order": 28,
        "source_type": "page"
      }
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "field": "multiplier_effects",
      "value": "Both tax cuts and spending have multiplier effects, in which the initial increase in demand from the policy percolates through the economy, generating additional economic activity.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": null,
      "metadata": {
        "chunk_id": "page_0029",
        "chunk_title": "Fiscal policy",
        "chunk_order": 28,
        "source_type": "page"
      }
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "field": "crowding_out",
      "value": "The effects of fiscal policy can be limited by crowding out.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": null,
      "metadata": {
        "chunk_id": "page_0029",
        "chunk_title": "Fiscal policy",
        "chunk_order": 28,
        "source_type": "page"
      }
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "field": "crowding_out_definition",
      "value": "When there is no output gap, the economy is producing at full capacity, with no excess productive resources.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": null,
      "metadata": {
        "chunk_id": "page_0029",
        "chunk_title": "Fiscal policy",
        "chunk_order": 28,
        "source_type": "page"
      }
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "field": "crowding_out_impact",
      "value": "If the government increases spending in this situation, it diverts resources that the private sector would otherwise use, so overall output does not increase.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": null,
      "metadata": {
        "chunk_id": "page_0029",
        "chunk_title": "Fiscal policy",
        "chunk_order": 28,
        "source_type": "page"
      }
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "field": "economists_opinion",
      "value": "Some economists think that crowding out is always an issue, while others do not think it is a major issue when output is depressed.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": null,
      "metadata": {
        "chunk_id": "page_0029",
        "chunk_title": "Fiscal policy",
        "chunk_order": 28,
        "source_type": "page"
      }
    },
    {
